.\"	@(#)touch.1	6.1 (Berkeley) 4/29/85
.\"
.TH TOUCH 1 "September 20, 2026"
.AT 3
.SH NAME
touch \- set file access and modification times
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B touch
[
.B \-acfm
]
[
.BI \-d " ccyy-mm-ddTHH:MM:SS[.frac][Z]"
]
[
.BI \-r " file"
]
[
.BI \-t " [[cc]yy]mmddHHMM[.SS]"
]
file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Touch
sets the access and modification times of each
.IR file .
The times are set through
.IR utimes (2)
and are recorded as whole seconds, which is the resolution the
filesystem keeps.
A
.I file
that does not exist is created empty, unless
.B \-c
is given.
.PP
The options are:
.TP 12
.B \-a
Change the access time.
.TP
.B \-c
Do not create a
.I file
that does not exist.
This is not an error.
.TP
.BI \-d " time"
Set the times to
.IR time ,
given as
.BR ccyy-mm-ddTHH:MM:SS[.frac][Z] .
A space may stand in place of the
.BR T .
A trailing
.B Z
reads the time as UTC rather than as local time.
Fractional seconds are accepted and discarded, the filesystem recording
whole seconds.
.TP
.B \-f
Accepted and ignored; it is kept for compatibility.
.TP
.B \-m
Change the modification time.
.TP
.BI \-r " file"
Set the times to those of
.IR file .
.TP
.BI \-t " time"
Set the times to
.IR time ,
given as
.BR [[cc]yy]mmddHHMM[.SS] .
A two-digit year in
.RB [ 00 , 68 ]
is read as 20xx and one in
.RB [ 69 , 99 ]
as 19xx.
.PP
Given neither
.B \-a
nor
.BR \-m ,
both times are changed.
Given one of them, the other time is read back and written unaltered,
because
.IR utimes (2)
sets both times or neither.
.PP
Given none of
.BR \-d ,
.B \-r
or
.BR \-t ,
the current time is used, and every
.I file
of one invocation is given the same instant.
.PP
If no such option is given and the first of two or more operands is an
eight or ten digit number, it is taken as a time in the obsolete form
.B mmddHHMM[yy]
rather than as a file name.
.SH EXIT\ STATUS
.I Touch
exits 0 if every
.I file
was set and >0 if any was not.
An operand that cannot be set does not stop the ones after it.
.SH SEE\ ALSO
utimes(2), stat(2), mktime(3)
.SH STANDARDS
.I Touch
conforms to IEEE Std 1003.1.
The
.B \-d
and
.B \-f
options are extensions to it.
.SH HISTORY
A
.B touch
command appeared in Version 7 AT&T UNIX and set the times by reading a
file's first byte and writing it back, which reached regular files alone
and could not set the two times apart.
This one sets them through
.IR utimes (2).
